Part of Cloudberry's greenplum_schedule on the port's cluster: the
differences from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and
are the port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test as
passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds no
difference or exactly the file's.

alter_distribution_policy  pg_appendonly's segrelid, which is 0: gp_ao keeps
  every table's segment files in gp_ao.segfile, and makes no relation of
  them for each table.  ALTER TABLE ... ALTER PARTITION FOR (1) SET WITH
  (appendonly = true) changes that partition's access method, which
  Cloudberry refuses, saying the partition is not partitioned.  And an
  INSERT ... SELECT into a randomly distributed table deals the rows round
  the segments: the port accepts gp.force_random_redistribution and
  redistributes as if it were on, where Cloudberry's planner, with it off,
  leaves each row on the segment it was read on.

alter_table_set_am  psql's \d+ footers of an append-optimized table, which
  are Cloudberry's psql's and not PostgreSQL 19's.  The segment files of a
  table ALTER TABLE ... SET ACCESS METHOD rewrites into gp_ao: numbered from
  1, where Cloudberry's rewrite writes segment 0, in gp_ao's own format,
  version 1, with the lengths of its 8K blocks.  And each segment's
  gp_toolkit.__gp_aoblkdir(): a directory row's segment file is the
  rewrite's, 1, and its tupleid, which the port's init_file masks, is its
  place in gp_ao.blkdir, one table of every table's directory rows, where
  Cloudberry's is in the table's own pg_aoblkdir relation.

am_encoding  psql's \d+ of a table by column, which PostgreSQL 19's psql
  prints without Cloudberry's columns for a column's compression and block
  size.  And a heap table with a column's ENCODING clause and compresstype
  in its WITH list, which both refuse: gp_ao refuses the ENCODING first,
  Cloudberry the option.

ao_locks (planner, orca)  the relations an append-optimized table's DELETE
  and UPDATE lock on a segment: Cloudberry's visibility map is a relation of
  each table's, with an index, and the port's is gp_ao.visimap, so only the
  table is locked there.  Under the planner the coordinator reads the rows
  through a gather, and holds AccessShareLock on the table beside the
  ExclusiveLock it writes under.

aocs (planner, orca)  aototal(), which sums the tuples of each table's
  pg_aoseg relation: gp_ao keeps segment files in gp_ao.segfile, so there is
  none to read, and the totals are 0; nor are there the indexes of each
  table's visibility map and block directory the test looks for.  PostgreSQL
  19's words for FETCH BACKWARD in a cursor that cannot scan backward, and
  psql's \d+ footer.  zlib's ratio over gp_ao's 8K blocks, 1.23 where
  Cloudberry's 32K ones give 1.26.  Under the planner a cursor's plan is a
  Sort of the gathered rows, which can scan backward, and pg_cursors says it
  is scrollable.

AORO_Compression  pg_relation_size(), the cluster's, of gp_ao's 8K pages:
  each node's file of a table has a page from the start, the coordinator's
  too, 32 kB for an empty table where Cloudberry's has 0 bytes; and zlib's
  ratio over gp_ao's 8K blocks.

as_alias  an ORDER BY of text, which puts "XXXX" before "bbbb": the cluster
  is made with the C locale, and Cloudberry's expected output with one that
  sorts letters before case.

bfv_dd, direct_dispatch, qp_targeted_dispatch (planner, orca)  the INFO
  lines of test_print_direct_dispatch_info and of its two-phase commit,
  which say where each statement and each command of a commit goes (see
  bfv_dd_multicolumn).  The port's planner reads each partition of a
  partitioned table through a gather of its own, a slice each, where
  Cloudberry reads the partitions in one; its INSERT and COPY send rows only
  to the segments their keys hash to; and a transaction that wrote on one
  segment commits there in one phase, where Cloudberry prepares it on every
  segment the transaction's statements went to.  A one-phase commit and a
  rollback before any part is prepared name every segment the transaction's
  statements went to, in the order they first went, as Cloudberry's do --
  so where a statement's slices go elsewhere than Cloudberry's, its commit's
  line differs with them.  Under ORCA, the slices
  below a write are numbered one higher than Cloudberry's: its write is its
  root slice, slice 0, and the port's is slice 1, below the coordinator's
  own.  The rows the statements return and change are Cloudberry's.

bfv_dd_multicolumn  the INFO lines of test_print_direct_dispatch_info that
  say where a statement goes.  Its INSERT ... SELECT of generate_series()
  runs the function in a slice of one segment, and its ANALYZE dispatches a
  slice, where the port's run the one on the coordinator and sample through
  O3.  The rest is Cloudberry's, under both planners: a key two columns of
  which an IN list or an OR fix is sent to the segments they hash to,
  "PARTIAL contents: 1 2", by the planner's gathers and by ORCA's Motions
  alike; an INSERT of one row of constants to the one segment it hashes to;
  and how each commits, in one phase on the segment an INSERT of one row
  wrote on.

bfv_joins  a join of twenty empty tables, which Cloudberry's demo cluster
  cancels for the memory its planner takes -- the runaway cleaner's red
  zone, M6's memory accounting -- and the port plans and answers, with no
  rows, in the time the test allows.  Its tables distributed with the legacy
  cdbhash_float4_ops class are joined as Cloudberry joins them.

bfv_subquery (planner, orca)  count_operator() finds no Seq Scan in the
  plan of a query over distributed tables: the port's planner reads each
  through a Gather Motion, which scans the table on the segments, and
  EXPLAIN names the Motion.  Under ORCA, the NOTICE of a ctid without its
  gp_segment_id in NOT IN (SELECT ctid FROM t_inner), which Cloudberry's
  planner gives and ORCA's plan does not -- Cloudberry's ORCA gives none
  either (bfv_subquery_optimizer.out).

bitmap_index  PostgreSQL 19's limit on an index row, a third of an 8K page,
  where Cloudberry's 32K pages take a larger one, refused on the segment the
  row goes to; and the HINT and the WARNING Cloudberry's fault injector gives
  with a PANIC, which name its FTS probe's fault, which the port's has not.

brin_ao, brin_aocs (planner, orca)  under the planner, the plans the test
  looks in for a Bitmap Heap Scan or a Seq Scan, which the segments make:
  the coordinator's plan is a gather of the table, and names neither.  Under
  ORCA, the comparisons of an inet or a cidr column and pg_lsn's IS NULL,
  which it plans without the BRIN index.  The rows are Cloudberry's.

case_gp  IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, and the CASE form, printed as the searched
  CASE the port's rewrite makes of them (see the singlenode suite's README).

qp_misc (orca)  the lines ORCA's fallback trace prints where Cloudberry's
  expected output has none, and nothing else: the test sets
  optimizer_trace_fallback, and the port's ORCA declines several DISTINCT
  aggregates, two subqueries that are not scalars, and one plan it finds
  none for; PostgreSQL's planner plans them, with the same answers.

DML_over_joins (orca)  the lines ORCA's fallback trace prints and nothing
  else: ORCA declines an UPDATE that moves a row of a partitioned table, a
  query of a multi-level partitioned one, a function it may not call on the
  segments (SIRV), and a table the coordinator alone has; the planner's
  answers are the same.  An UPDATE or DELETE that reads another table, or of
  a partitioned table, is ORCA's where its target is held in ExclusiveLock,
  as without the global deadlock detector it is; so is one that compares
  with a subquery's aggregate, which ORCA gathers to one segment.

expand_table_ao  pg_appendonly's compresstype of a table made with
  compresstype=none, which gp_ao records as no compression at all, an empty
  name, where Cloudberry records "none"; and the OID of text's hash operator
  class (see gp_create_table).

external_table (planner, orca)  an external table's dependency on its
  custom protocol, which DROP PROTOCOL finds from the tables' locations
  (gp_exttable's protocol.c), where Cloudberry records it in pg_depend
  against pg_extprotocol, a catalog of its own; and psql's \d of an
  external table, which PostgreSQL 19's psql prints with the "Server:" line
  of any foreign table's, gp_exttable_server's.  The rows a query's scans
  rejected, as the NOTICE counts them: an initplan's counted with the rest,
  where Cloudberry, which runs it as a dispatch of its own before the query,
  leaves them out; and under the planner a merge join whose outer side is
  empty never reads its inner side, a third scan of the table, where
  Cloudberry's slices all run and read their tables (ORCA's plan reads all
  three, as Cloudberry's does).  And a PL/pgSQL function's INSERT that fails
  on a segment: the context says where in the function, as the
  coordinator's context callbacks say it for any error, where Cloudberry,
  which rethrows the segment's error as the INSERT finishes, leaves them out.

external_table_create_privs  the rights CREATEEXTTABLE gives a role, which
  gp_exttable keeps in the role's gp_exttable security label
  ("gpfdist:r,gpfdist:w,http:r"): pg_authid has no rolcreaterextgpfd,
  rolcreaterexthttp or rolcreatewextgpfd column to read them from.  What
  the role may then make, and what is refused, are Cloudberry's.

external_table_persistent_error_log (orca)  the lines ORCA's fallback trace
  prints for gp_truncate_error_log() and gp_truncate_persistent_error_log()
  called without a FROM, functions that change what they read, which ORCA
  declines there ("SIRV functions") as Cloudberry's ORCA does -- its own
  expected output for ORCA has them.

gp_backend_info  the backends of a query that needs a reader gang, and
  the singleton reader on the coordinator: the port's gather reads each
  table through the segment's one connection, and joins them here, so
  after the join of temp and temp2 and the one of temp and pg_class the
  function lists the coordinator's backend and the writers alone, where
  Cloudberry's lists a reader of each segment's too and an entry-db
  reader.  Before those, what it lists is Cloudberry's: one backend for
  the coordinator, then one writer on each segment.

gp_create_table  the OID of text's hash operator class in
  gp_distribution_policy.distclass: the same class, which Cloudberry's
  catalog numbers 10039 and PostgreSQL 19's 10037.

gp_dqa (orca)  ORCA's fallback trace, which the expected output closest to
  the pass, the planner's, does not print, for the queries it declines and
  the planner answers the same: several DISTINCT aggregates, where
  optimizer_enable_multiple_distinct_aggs is off, as Cloudberry's ORCA
  declines them; and an aggregate with FILTER.

gp_metadata  version(), which is PostgreSQL 19's and names no Apache
  Cloudberry: the port is PostgreSQL's server, with its modules.
  gp_opt_version() answers in Cloudberry's words.

gp_types  PostgreSQL 19's words for an operator no type takes: a DETAIL
  where Cloudberry's HINT stood.

gpctas (orca)  the lines ORCA's fallback trace prints and nothing else,
  the test setting optimizer_trace_fallback: catalogs, a column of a
  non-default collation, functions called without a FROM, a ROW expression
  and a subquery that is not a scalar, which Cloudberry's ORCA declines in
  its own expected output too.  A REFRESH, whose query the port's planner
  plans, says nothing where Cloudberry's ORCA says it declines one.

gpdist_legacy_opclasses  the complex type, Cloudberry's own, which
  PostgreSQL 19 has not got: the table whose key takes every legacy
  cdbhash_*_ops class has a column of it, and is not made.  The other
  classes are gp_core's, and a table keyed on all of them -- uuid's in
  complex's place -- takes them, holds a row and finds it; the rows the test
  places on the segments are where Cloudberry places them.

join_gp  the NOTICE of test_print_prefetch_joinqual: where a join's outer
  side and its join qual both read through a Motion, Cloudberry reads the
  qual's rows first, against a deadlock between the two, and says so;
  PostgreSQL's joins, the port's, never do.  Its table of one segment,
  which gp_debug_numsegments makes, is read there alone, and answers as
  Cloudberry's does.

matview_ao  psql's \dm, whose title PostgreSQL 19's psql gives as "List of
  materialized views" where Cloudberry's psql says "List of relations".

part_external_table (orca)  the lines ORCA's fallback trace prints for a
  partitioned table with external partitions: the dynamic foreign scan ORCA
  plans for it the port's ORCA does not translate
  (TranslateDXLDynForeignScan), and the planner plans the query; and an
  INSERT into one, which Cloudberry's ORCA declines too.

partition_indexing  a UNIQUE index or constraint on a partitioned table
  that holds neither the partition key nor the distribution key: both
  refuse it, Cloudberry by its distribution key first, the port by
  PostgreSQL 19's check of the partition key, which DefineIndex() makes as
  it builds the index, before gp_sql checks the indexes a statement made
  against the distribution (distribution.c); and PostgreSQL 19's word for
  it, UNIQUE, where PostgreSQL 16's was unique.

partition_prune_opfamily (planner, orca)  its first EXPLAIN, which
  gpdiff.pl compares as rows: a comment line of dashes in the test reads to
  it as the rule under a result's header, and what follows, to the plan's
  "(7 rows)", is sorted as one result.  Under the planner the port's plan is
  an Append of a Gather Motion on each partition, where Cloudberry gathers
  one Append; under ORCA it is ORCA's Dynamic Seq Scan, as Cloudberry's ORCA
  plans it (partition_prune_opfamily_optimizer.out), with the partitions'
  scans, which the port's EXPLAIN prints beneath it.  The rows are
  Cloudberry's.

partition_pruning  the bitmap index (M5): three indexes are not made, and
  a DROP FUNCTION ... CASCADE finds none of them to drop.  The rest, its
  partitions pruned under both planners, is Cloudberry's.

partition_storage, uao_ddl_gp_partition_tables_column,
  uao_ddl_gp_partition_tables_row  psql's \d+ of append-optimized and
  column-oriented tables and partitions, which PostgreSQL 19's psql prints
  without Cloudberry's columns and footers of their storage, and with their
  NOT NULL constraints, which PostgreSQL 19 makes constraints.  A
  partitioned table's storage options, which PostgreSQL 19 refuses in
  pg_class.reloptions and gp_ao keeps in a label of its own: its reloptions
  and psql's Options: footer are empty.

qp_dml_joins  the WARNING of an ALTER TABLE ... SET DISTRIBUTED BY (b) of a
  table CREATE TABLE AS made distributed by b already: the port gives a
  table made from a query its first column as its key, where Cloudberry's
  planner gives it the key its rows arrive by.

qp_functions (planner, orca)  PostgreSQL 19's words for a call no function
  takes -- a DETAIL where Cloudberry's HINT stood -- and for a SQL function's
  last statement, which may be a MERGE ... RETURNING now.  Cloudberry refuses
  two queries whose SQL function reads a table in a slice it cannot: under
  the planner the port gathers fun_tree's rows and calls child_list() on the
  coordinator, where it reads the table whole, and answers; under ORCA the
  call is in the segments' slice and refused as Cloudberry refuses it.
  is_invalid_name() reads a replicated table, which Cloudberry's slice on the
  coordinator's entry database has no copy of; the port has no such slice,
  calls it in the coordinator's own plan, and answers in both passes, from
  the relations named tst% in its group's cluster -- a table an earlier test
  leaves there would be among them, as alter_distribution_policy's tstab was
  before the suite ran in groups.

qp_subquery (orca)  the fallback trace of the queries ORCA's translator
  declines -- subqueries that are not scalar, an inherited table, a
  whole-row variable -- which the planner answers the same.

rangefuncs_cdb (planner, orca)  the planner's settings the test lists,
  enable_*: PostgreSQL 19's, which has enable_distinct_reordering and four
  more that PostgreSQL 14 had not, and none of Cloudberry's own --
  enable_groupagg, enable_lock_optimization, enable_password_profile --
  with PostgreSQL's defaults, where Cloudberry turns off merge joins,
  nested loops and incremental sorts.  Under the planner, notice_srf(), a
  volatile set-returning function the rows of an INSERT come from, is
  called on the coordinator, where Cloudberry's planner calls it in a slice
  on one segment, so its NOTICE names segment -1.

resource_queue  SHOW resource_scheduler, whose header is the port's name
  of it, gp.resource_scheduler, three letters wider.  And the NOTICE of the
  SPI memory reservation of the Result node that calls pg_sleep() in the
  extended-protocol client's query: Cloudberry reserves an operator's share
  of the statement's memory for the queries it runs through SPI, and the
  port's statement runs with its budget as its work_mem, and gives no
  operator a share of its own.

statement_mem_for_windowagg  AVG(DISTINCT x) OVER (...), which PostgreSQL
  19 refuses: "DISTINCT is not implemented for window functions".
  Cloudberry's WindowAgg sorts each partition's values for DISTINCT, in a
  sort its statement_mem sizes, which the test's second half measures, and
  NOTICEs from its fault distinct_winagg_perform_sort; PostgreSQL 19 has
  no such sort to size.  The first half is Cloudberry's: a window's
  tuplestore in the memory statement_mem gives the statement, as its
  work_mem, in memory at 4 MB and spilled at 1 MB, whose plans the suite
  does not compare.

returning_gp, triggers_gp, update_gp  what Cloudberry refuses on an
  append-optimized table, or on a table another inherits from, and the port
  carries out: DELETE ... RETURNING of an append-optimized table, a row
  trigger on one ("feature not supported on appendoptimized relations"), and
  an UPDATE of the distribution key of an inherited table ("can't split
  update for inherit table"), whose plan the port prints.

rpt (planner, orca)  the plans of writes to a replicated table that compute
  a volatile function -- random() in a condition, in a CREATE TABLE AS's
  HAVING -- which Cloudberry cannot devise, since each segment would
  compute a value of its own: the port computes it once, on the
  coordinator, and sends every segment the same rows (gp_explicit.c).
  Under ORCA, too, the trace of its falling back on a replicated table
  while the test has optimizer_enable_replicated_table off, and three
  plans of volatile subqueries ORCA's own rules move lines of.

rpt_joins  the plan of a data-modifying WITH query under a correlated
  subquery, which Cloudberry's planner cannot devise and PostgreSQL 19's
  plans.

sequence_gp  nextval() over gp_dist_random('gp_id'), which Cloudberry
  calls on each segment, and each segment takes values from a chunk of the
  sequence of its own, 20 long: 1, 21 and 41, and 61 on the coordinator.
  The port gathers gp_id's rows and calls it on the coordinator, which
  takes 1, 2 and 3, then 4.  No value twice in either, which is what the
  test is for.

sreh  COPY's SEGMENT REJECT LIMIT, reached: the port's COPY parses each row
  on the coordinator, where the rows it rejects are counted, and reaches the
  limit at the second bad row, line 3; Cloudberry's coordinator parses a
  row's distribution key alone, and reached it at the second bad key, line
  11, before the segments, which parse the rest, reached theirs.

table_statistics  the position PostgreSQL 19 gives the error of an ALTER
  TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE of a partition key column, a LINE and its
  caret, which PostgreSQL 16's gives none.

truncate_gp  the files stat_table_segfile_size(), a PL/Python function,
  finds of an append-optimized and a column-oriented table emptied by a
  TRUNCATE in the transaction that made them: gp_ao keeps a table's segment
  files as extents of pages in its relation, whose block 0 is its metapage,
  so the table is one file of one page, 8192 bytes, where Cloudberry's files
  are empty; and a table by column keeps its columns there too, where
  Cloudberry's has a file of each, numbered from 128.  The heap table's
  files are Cloudberry's on every segment.

uao_ddl_alter_ao_part_exch_column, uao_ddl_alter_ao_part_exch_row
  gp.select_invisible, which is gp_ao's and shows the rows an
  append-optimized table's visibility map hides; Cloudberry's
  gp_select_invisible shows a heap table's dead rows too, and counts the
  three the test's UPDATE of its heap partition left.

uao_ddl_create_ao_tables_column, uao_ddl_create_ao_tables_row  the relations
  pg_appendonly names for a table's segment files, visibility map and block
  directory, and their indexes, which gp_ao keeps in its own tables,
  gp_ao.segfile, visimap and blkdir: pg_appendonly names none.

uao_ddl_temp_on_commit_delete_rows_column,
  uao_ddl_temp_on_commit_delete_rows_row  the segment file rows are written
  to in the transaction that made the table: 1, as gp_ao numbers them from
  1, where Cloudberry writes segment 0, which it keeps for a table made in
  the same transaction and for utility mode.

uaocs_catalog_tables  what gp_toolkit.__gp_aovisimap_hidden_info() finds
  after VACUUM FULL, which gp_ao carries out as CLUSTER rewrites a table,
  into a new segment file 1 on each segment; Cloudberry's compacts a segment
  file with deleted rows into a new one, 2, and lists the files it emptied
  as well.

vacuum_full_ao  pg_relation_size() of pg_appendonly's segrelid on segment
  1: gp_ao keeps segment files in gp_ao.segfile, so segrelid is 0, and the
  size of no relation is null.

vacuum_full_heap_bitmapindex  the table's size on segment 1, of 8K pages,
  122880 bytes where Cloudberry's 32K pages make 131072.

with_clause  three recursive queries Cloudberry refuses -- a join of nested
  RECURSIVE clauses, and window functions in a recursive query's target
  list -- which PostgreSQL 19 answers.
